window.docContents[72].resolve({"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates--Homogeneous-planar-profiles":{"contents":"In two dimensions the angular variable can be kept explicit by adding the\nangular term  to the radial operator.\n\nFor a two-variable profile , define the polar differential\nexpressionAt every , a homogeneous angular mode satisfiesConsequently, the angular equation\n produces the polar source\n. More generally, if\n and  is a radial  cutoff,\nLean proves the product rule with every cutoff error explicit:For a Cartesian  field , Lean additionally proves, at every\n,The parameter-plane operator is proved equal to the curried operator\n, and a germ-local theorem permits replacing\n by a locally equal polar profile. Thus the homogeneous and cutoff calculations become Euclidean\nLaplacian identities once their  pullback hypotheses hold.\n\nThe radial-power formula with dimension two gives\n. The angular derivative\ncontributes . Set , negate, and collect the\ncommon factor . The radial product rule then expands\n and isolates the two terms containing\n or . For the coordinate bridge, apply the second-order\nchain rule to ; the first-order terms cancel, the mixed Hessian\nterms cancel, and  leaves the Cartesian trace.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Euclidean, polar, and spherical coordinates","header":"3.3.1.4. Homogeneous planar profiles","id":"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates--Homogeneous-planar-profiles"},"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Other-PDE-libraries-and-geometric-flow":{"contents":"Soto Franco and Marshall-Stevens's\nEllipticPDE\nformalizes linear divergence-form Dirichlet theory\nwith a companion paper.\nIts named results cover weak solvability, Gårding's inequality, Fredholm theory,\nspectral compactness, and interior regularity. This overlaps directly with\nPDEAtlas's variational and regularity layers. Their work concentrates on linear\nelliptic solvability, while PDEAtlas follows a wider textbook progression and\nconnects that analysis to evolution equations and certified calculations.\nThe useful compatibility questions concern their Sobolev-space definitions,\ncoefficient assumptions, and the passage from a weak solution to a regular\nrepresentative. A Hölder conclusion obtained from smooth-coefficient Sobolev\nregularity has different hypotheses from De Giorgi–Nash–Moser theory.\n\nQin, Liao, Khaitan and Chow's\nPoincaré formalization\nfollows Hamilton–Perelman geometric analysis:\ntheir paper describes Ricci flow with surgery\nand finite-time extinction leading to the smooth and topological Poincaré\nconjectures. This is PDE formalization in a geometric setting, with evolving\nmetrics and curvature. PDEAtlas's present exposition concerns Euclidean-domain\nanalysis. Sharing estimates, compactness or evolution arguments would require\nconnecting our Euclidean objects to the geometric objects and hypotheses of\nthat project.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Related Work","header":"2.2. Other PDE libraries and geometric flow","id":"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Other-PDE-libraries-and-geometric-flow"}});